# Single Responsibility

Every module, function, and class should have **one reason to change**. Keep
unrelated concerns — HTTP transport, business rules, data access, filesystem,
logging — in separate units that delegate to each other. If you need "and" to
describe what a unit does, split it.

## Separate transport, domain, and data access

The `functions/` backend is the reference split — mirror it in new code:

- **Handler** (`functions/src/functions/*/index.ts`) — maps HTTP ⇄ domain only:
  parse the request, call the domain function, translate result/errors to status
  codes. No business rules, no Firestore.
- **Domain** (`functions/src/functions/*/function.ts`) — business rules only;
  knows nothing about HTTP or Firestore APIs.
- **Store** (`functions/src/services/repositories-store.ts`) — the only place
  that touches Firestore; each method is one query or mutation.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — one function fetches, writes files, AND cleans up on error
async function add(repoUrl, skills) {
  const repoFiles = await githubService.getRepoFiles(...); // transport
  writeRepoFiles(repoFiles, '.agents');                    // filesystem
  await backendService.deleteRepoInfo(repoUrl);            // cleanup leaks in
}

// ✅ GOOD — thin handler delegates the rule to the domain function
// functions/src/functions/store-repo-info/index.ts
export const apiStoreRepoInfo = onRequest({ invoker: 'public' }, async (request, response) => {
  try {
    const id = await storeRepoInfo(request.body);
    response.status(201).send({ id });
  } catch (error) {
    logError('Failed to write repository', { error });
    response.status(400).send({ error: 'Invalid repository payload' });
  }
});

// ✅ GOOD — domain owns one rule, delegates persistence to the store
// functions/src/functions/store-repo-info/function.ts
export async function storeRepoInfo(input: unknown): Promise<string> {
  const data = repositorySchema.parse(input);
  const repo = await repositoriesStore.findByPath(data.repoSlug);
  return repo ? repo.id : repositoriesStore.add(data);
}
```

## Checklist

- A unit has one reason to change; its description needs no "and".
- Transport, domain rules, and data access live in separate units.
- Handlers hold no business logic; stores hold no business rules.
- No stray debugging or unrelated cleanup inside a unit.
